tor","content":"Cycle-Accurate Simulator"},"1382":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-5","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#use-cases","content":"Use Cases"},"1383":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-6","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#citation","content":"Citation"},"1384":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-7","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","content":"**Compute-in-Memory (CIM)** is a computing paradigm where data processing occurs directly within memory arrays, eliminating the energy-intensive data movement between separate memory and processing units. SRAM-based CIM accelerators have emerged as promising solutions for neural network inference, offering significant improvements in energy efficiency and throughput compared to conventional architectures."},"1385":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-8","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","content":"CIMFlow addresses the challenge of evaluating CIM accelerator designs before hardware implementation. The framework provides an integrated toolchain that compiles neural network models to CIM-specific instructions and simulates their execution with cycle-accurate timing."},"1386":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-9","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#two-level-compiler","content":"The compiler separates workload partitioning from code generation, enabling optimization at both the graph and operator levels."},"1387":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-10","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#instruction-set-architecture","content":"CIMFlow defines a flexible 32-bit ISA with hierarchical hardware abstraction spanning chip, core, and unit levels."},"1388":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-11","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#cycle-accurate-simulator","content":"The simulator provides cycle-accurate performance analysis through detailed modeling of the digital CIM architecture."},"1389":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-12","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#use-cases","content":"CIMFlow enables rapid evaluation of CIM configurations by varying parameters such as macro group configuration (T, K), core count, memory capacity, and NoC bandwidth to understand their impact on performance and energy efficiency for specific workloads."},"1390":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-13","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#use-cases","content":"The framework provides detailed performance metrics for neural network models on CIM hardware, enabling comparison of models, quantization schemes, and mapping strategies to identify optimal deployment configurations."},"1391":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-14","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#use-cases","content":"CIMFlow tracks energy consumption across different architectural components, enabling hardware designers to evaluate energy efficiency and make informed decisions before committing to silicon implementation."},"1392":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-15","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#use-cases","content":"The modular architecture supports experimentation with new compilation strategies, enabling development and evaluation of custom optimization passes for workload partitioning, memory management, and instruction scheduling."},"1393":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-16","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#citation","content":"CIMFlow was presented at the 62nd ACM/IEEE Design Automation Conference (DAC 2025). If you use CIMFlow in your research, please cite:"},"1394":{"id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow-17","page_id":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Framework/what-is-cimflow#citation","content":"The full paper is available on arXiv."},"1395":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","type":"page","content":"Architecture","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Simulator","Internals"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture"},"1396":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-0","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","content":"Static module composition and source-map of CIMFlow-Simulator"},"1397":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-1","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#chip-level-and-core-level","content":"Chip Level and Core Level"},"1398":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-2","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#execute-units","content":"Execute Units"},"1399":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-3","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#source-directory-map","content":"Source Directory Map"},"1400":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-4","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","content":"The simulator runtime is organized in three levels: `Chip → Core → Units`.\nThis page focuses on static composition and ownership boundaries."},"1401":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-5","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#chip-level-and-core-level","content":"The two outermost levels define the overall simulation topology."},"1402":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-6","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#chip-level-and-core-level","content":"At chip construction, each core receives its instruction stream and connects its local switch to the shared network. The global memory switch is also bound to the same network."},"1403":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-7","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#chip-level-and-core-level","content":"Each `Core` contains the following subcomponents:"},"1404":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-8","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#execute-units","content":"Each core contains six specialized execute units:"},"1405":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-9","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#execute-units","content":"Branch and jump targets are resolved in the decoder. `TAG`/`WAIT` are decoded as control opcodes but executed by `TransferUnit`."},"1406":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-10","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#source-directory-map","content":"The tree below shows the source directory layout of the CIMFlow-Simulator C++ codebase. Each directory corresponds to a major subsystem documented in this section."},"1407":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-11","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture#source-directory-map","content":"<File name=\"cim_simulator.cpp\" />"},"1408":{"id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture-12","page_id":"/docs/Simulator/Architecture","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Simula