,"page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#addressing-modes","content":"The opcode bits `X` and `Y` control immediate offset addressing for source and destination:"},"1920":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-8","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"The `MEM_CPY` instruction uses optional flags to enable offset addressing for source and/or destination addresses:"},"1921":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-9","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"**Flag Combinations:**"},"1922":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-10","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"Flags"},"1923":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-11","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"Opcode"},"1924":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-12","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"Source Address"},"1925":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-13","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"Destination Address"},"1926":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-14","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"(none)"},"1927":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-15","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"110000"},"1928":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-16","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"GRF\\[rs]"},"1929":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-17","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"GRF\\[rd]"},"1930":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-18","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"`DST_O`"},"1931":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-19","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"110001"},"1932":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-20","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"GRF\\[rs]"},"1933":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-21","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"GRF\\[rd] + imm"},"1934":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-22","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"`SRC_O`"},"1935":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-23","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"110010"},"1936":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-24","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"GRF\\[rs] + imm"},"1937":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-25","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"GRF\\[rd]"},"1938":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-26","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"`SRC_O, DST_O`"},"1939":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-27","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"110011"},"1940":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-28","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"GRF\\[rs] + imm"},"1941":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-29","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"GRF\\[rd] + imm"},"1942":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement-30","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Data-Movement#operation-flags","content":"The transfer size is specified in bytes via `GRF[rt]`. When both flags are set, the same immediate offset value is used for both source and destination addresses."},"1943":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","type":"page","content":"Inter-Core Communication","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Abstraction","ISA","Instruction Design","Instruction Set"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication"},"1944":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication-0","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","content":"Cross-core data transmission and synchronization instructions"},"1945":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication-1","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication#send","content":"SEND"},"1946":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication-2","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication#recv","content":"RECV"},"1947":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication-3","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication#examples","content":"Examples"},"1948":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication-4","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","content":"Inter-core communication instructions enable data exchange and synchronization between processing cores in a multi-core architecture. These instructions use communication IDs to ensure reliable, ordered message passing."},"1949":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication-5","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication#send","content":"Initiates a data transmission from the current core to a remote receiver core. The instruction specifies sender/receiver IDs, destination address, transfer size, and a communication ID for acknowledgment tracking."},"1950":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication-6","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication#recv","content":"Receives data from a remote sender core. The instruction specifies sender ID, source/destination addresses, transfer size, and a communication ID that must match the corresponding SEND operation."},"1951":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication-7","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Communication#recv","content":"Communication IDs must match between SEND and RECV operations. Mismatched IDs may cause deadlock or data corruption."},"1952":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","type":"page","content":"Inter-Core Synchronization","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Abstraction","ISA","Instruction Design","Instruction Set"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization"},"1953":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-0","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","content":"Multi-core synchronization and barrier instructions"},"1954":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-1","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#wait","content":"WAIT"},"1955":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-2","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#barrier","content":"BARRIER"},"1956":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-3","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#tag","content":"TAG"},"1957":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-4","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#examples","content":"Examples"},"1958":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-5","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","content":"Inter-core synchronization instructions provide fine-grained coordination primitives for multi-core systems, enabling both pairwise synchronization and collective barrier operations across multiple cores."},"1959":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-6","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#wait","content":"Performs pairwise synchronization between cores. The current core blocks until a specified synchronization point is reached, with flexible control over which core(s) to wait for and expected write count."},"1960":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-7","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#wait","content":"WAIT works in conjunction with TAG for producer-consumer synchronization. When GRF\\[rs] is 0, WAIT blocks until global synchronization; when set to a specific core ID, it waits for that core's TAG."},"1961":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-8","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#barrier","content":"Performs a collective synchronization across multiple cores using a barrier protocol. All participating cores must reach the barrier before any can proceed, ensuring global consistency in multi-core computations."},"1962":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-9","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#barrier","content":"All cores must execute BARRIER with identical `num_cores` and `barrier_id` values. Mismatched parameters will cause deadlock."},"1963":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-10","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#tag","content":"Marks a synchronization point on the current core, signaling completion of work to other cores waiting via WAIT instruction."},"1964":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization-11","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Inter-Core-Synchronization#tag","content":"TAG is used in conjunction with WAIT to implement producer-consumer synchronization patterns. A core signals completion of a phase by executing TAG, and other cores unblock by using WAIT with the same sync ID."},"1965":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","type":"page","content":"Intra-Core Control","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Abstraction","ISA","Instruction Design","Instruction Set"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control"},"1966":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-0","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","content":"Control flow instructions for branching and jumping"},"1967":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-1","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#branch","content":"BRANCH"},"1968":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-2","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#branch-variants","content":"Branch Variants"},"1969":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-3","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#jmp","content":"JMP"},"1970":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-4","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#examples","content":"Examples"},"1971":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-5","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","content":"Intra-core control instructions manage program execution flow within a single core, providing both conditional branching based on register comparisons and unconditional jumps using PC-relative addressing."},"1972":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-6","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#branch","content":"Performs a conditional jump based on comparison of two register values. The instruction supports four comparison types encoded in the opcode, enabling efficient decision-making for loops, conditionals, and control flow."},"1973":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-7","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#branch-variants","content":"The opcode bits `XX` specify the comparison operation:"},"1974":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-8","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#branch-variants","content":"Branch offset is PC-relative: `target = PC + imm`. The offset is measured in instruction units from the next instruction."},"1975":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-9","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#jmp","content":"Performs an unconditional jump to a target address specified by a PC-relative offset. Unlike BRANCH, JMP always transfers control regardless of any condition."},"1976":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control-10","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Intra-Core-Control#jmp","content":"JMP provides a larger offset range (26 bits) compared to BRANCH (16 bits), enabling jumps across larger code sections."},"1977":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","type":"page","content":"Matrix–Vector Operation","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Abstraction","ISA","Instruction Design","Instruction Set"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation"},"1978":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation-0","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","content":"Computing-in-memory matrix-vector multiplication instructions"},"1979":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation-1","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation#cim_mvm","content":"CIM_MVM"},"1980":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation-2","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation#operation-flags","content":"Operation Flags"},"1981":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation-3","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation#examples","content":"Examples"},"1982":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation-4","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","content":"Matrix–vector operations leverage the CIM array for efficient parallel multiplication between weight matrices and input vectors, enabling high-performance deep learning inference."},"1983":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation-5","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation#cim_mvm","content":"Performs matrix-vector multiplication (MVM) using the CIM array, computing the product of a weight matrix stored in CIM and an input feature vector from local memory. Supports batch operations via configurable flag bits."},"1984":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation-6","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation#operation-flags","content":"The `flags` field controls execution modes for CIM matrix-vector multiplication. Multiple flags can be combined to optimize computation."},"1985":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation-7","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Matrix-Vector-Operation#operation-flags","content":"**Accumulation (ACC)** is always enabled by default - results are accumulated to the output buffer. This flag is implicit and not exposed in assembly syntax."},"1986":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Scalar-Operation","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Scalar-Operation","type":"page","content":"Scalar Operation","breadcrumbs":["Documentation","Abstraction","ISA","Instruction Design","Instruction Set"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Scalar-Operation"},"1987":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Scalar-Operation-0","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Scalar-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Scalar-Operation","content":"Scalar arithmetic, loa