s enable Single Instruction, Multiple Data (SIMD) processing for efficient parallel computation on arrays. These instructions support element-wise operations, activation functions, quantization, and aggregation across vector elements."},"2023":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation-8","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation#vec_op","content":"Performs SIMD operations on 1-4 input vectors, applying the same operation to corresponding elements in parallel. Supports various functions including activation functions (ReLU), quantization, and arithmetic operations."},"2024":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation-9","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation#input-vector-modes","content":"In the instruction syntax, `Z` determines the number of input vectors.\n`Z` corresponds to the opcode bits `XY`:"},"2025":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation-10","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation#input-vector-modes","content":"For operations with >2 input vectors, addresses for vectors 3 and 4 are sourced from dedicated vector registers configured separately."},"2026":{"id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation-11","page_id":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/Abstraction/Instruction-Design/Instruction-Set/Vector-Operation#function-codes","content":"Complete list of SIMD vector operation function codes (funct field) as defined in the implementati