he output is always `final_code.json` regardless of the internal pass ordering."],[743,"The CG instructions are organized by core and stage:"],[17,"The chip level describes the top-level organization of a multi-core CIM accelerator, including how cores communicate and share resources."],[24,"The CIM unit performs matrix-vector multiplication (MVM) in SRAM arrays. It contains **K** macro groups, each with **T** macros (both are architecture parameters)."],[1152,"The CIMFlow compiler transforms ONNX models into optimized instruction sequences through a two-level pipeline."],[37,"The CIMFlow ISA defines the programming interface for SRAM-based Compute-in-Memory accelerators. It maps neural network operations to hardware through a hierarchical abstraction model, enabling efficient compilation and execution of deep learning workloads."],[738,"The compilation flow consists of three main stages, each producing inspectable intermediate outputs:"],[223,"The compiler accepts the following hardware parameters."],[132,"The compiler can be invoked through `cimflow compile` for standard workflows or the `cim-compiler` native binary for advanced use."],[412,"The Compiler section documents `CIMFlow-Compiler`, the compiler that converts ONNX neural network models into ISA instruction streams for the CIMFlow simulator."],[1386,"The compiler separates workload partitioning from code generation, enabling optimization at both the graph and operator levels."],[1155,"The Computational Graph level handles workload partitioning:"],[579,"The container working directory structure:"],[1450,"The core runtime follows a four-stage pipeline:\nInstruction Fetch (`IF`) → Instruction Decode (`ID`) → Instruction Schedule (`IS`) → Instruction Execute (`IE`).\nDifferent instructions can occupy different stages concurrently."],[1456,"The core stalls when either condition holds:"],[1453,"The decoder and PC update operate on separate phase offsets within each cycle, allowing instruction decode and issue to