marker and `WAIT` as a pairwise synchronization primitive (see Inter-Core Synchronization for the architectural specification). In the current simulator runtime, these are implemented as follows:"],[756,"The ISA instructions use numeric opcodes:"],[38,"The ISA models CIM hardware at three abstraction levels:"],[763,"The JSON format is machine-readable but verbose. Convert to assembly for easier inspection:"],[275,"The low-level backend is invoked via the `cim-compiler compile` command. It is used directly for DSL programs and indirectly during OP-level kernel generation."],[400,"The Model Simplify stage performs ONNX preprocessing, including operator fusion (e.g., BatchNorm folding) and shape inference. The CG IR output (`instructions_<...>.json`) serves directly as OP-level input — when running the two-step process, the CG output directory becomes the OP input path."],[1392,"The modular architecture supports experimentation with new compilation strategies, enabling development and evaluation of custom optimization passes for workload partitioning, memory management, and instruction scheduling."],[180,"The native `cim-compiler` binary provides additional commands and options not exposed through `cimflow compile`."],[194,"The native `cim-compiler` uses uppercase flags (`-T`, `-K`, `-B`, `-C`) while `cimflow compile` uses lowercase (`-t`, `-k`, `-b`, `-c`)."],[1535,"The network matrix file must exist at the configured path"],[1528,"The number of core entries must equal `core_cnt`"],[1919,"The opcode bits `X` and `Y` control immediate offset addressing for source and destination:"],[1973,"The opcode bits `XX` specify the comparison operation:"],[1160,"The Operator level generates executable instructions:"],[1317,"The output directory contains simulation results including performance metrics."],[643,"The Pipeline Command"],[699,"The pipeline executed three stages:"],[740,"The progress bar shows dynamic programming solving the optimal graph-to-stage mapping. Each \"prefi